# apparmor — confine programs to a limited set of resources

AppArmor protects the system by confining programs to a limited set of resources. Profiles live in `/etc/apparmor.d/`. It ships enabled on OpenSUSE Tumbleweed.

## Check overall status and loaded profiles

```sh
sudo aa-status
# or
sudo apparmor_status
```

## Activate (enforce) a profile

```sh
sudo aa-enforce usr.bin.firefox
```

## Set a profile to complain mode (log violations, allow them)

```sh
sudo aa-complain usr.bin.firefox
```

## Disable a profile

```sh
sudo aa-disable usr.bin.firefox
```

## Real-world: Plex blocked by AppArmor on Tumbleweed

```sh
# See recent denials
sudo journalctl -k | grep -i apparmor | tail -20

# Loosen just the offending profile to complain mode while testing
sudo aa-complain plexmediaserver

# Re-enforce once the profile is fixed
sudo aa-enforce plexmediaserver
```

## Real-world: reload a profile after editing

```sh
sudo apparmor_parser -r /etc/apparmor.d/usr.sbin.smbd
```

| Tool             | Purpose                          |
| ---------------- | -------------------------------- |
| aa-status        | list profiles + modes            |
| aa-enforce       | enforce a profile                |
| aa-complain      | log-only mode                    |
| aa-disable       | unload + disable a profile       |
| apparmor_parser  | load/replace/remove profiles     |
